# PARKINSON_NET_INFO’ Chatbot - a Digital Information Support System for Stakeholders in Parkinson's Disease Therapy

## Project Team

Professor of the Department, Doctor of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Professor Glembotskaya G.T.; Assistant, PhD student Kristeleva D.A.; Doctor of Medical Sciences, Professor of the Department of Nervous Diseases Shindryaeva N.N.

## Project Description

## Project Aim

to develop the digital chatbot “PARKINSON_NET_INFO” providing information support to physicians, pharmacy professionals and patients with Parkinson's disease and aimed at improving the quality of pharmacotherapy and medicines supply using modern digital technologies.

## Key Objectives

- to develop a framework for digital information support for physicians, pharmacy professionals and patients with Parkinson's disease;
- to ensure that patients receive timely information on pharmacotherapy and medicines supply;
- to develop tools for collecting and assessing data on changes in patients' condition over time;
- to support the identification of patients at risk of cognitive impairment to enable timely referral and follow-up;
- to provide information support to physicians in pharmacotherapy assessment and medication selection;
- to enable improvement of the recording and monitoring of antiparkinsonian medication dispensing and the analysis of medicines supply;
- to develop approaches to integrating the “PARKINSON_NET_INFO” chatbot into the digital healthcare infrastructure, including the electronic medical record, the patient portal and telemedicine services;
- to identify prospects for implementation and commercialization of the solution in healthcare organizations and digital healthcare environments.

## Key Publications

1. KRISTELEVA D.A., GLEMBOTSKAYA G.T., SHINDRYAEVA N.N. Pharmacotherapy in Parkinson's disease. Current Problems of Health Care and Medical Statistics. 2025. No. 5. pp. 31-42. (In Russ.)
2. KRISTELEVA D.A., GLEMBOTSKAYA G.T., SHINDRYAEVA N.N. A comprehensive approach to assessing pharmacotherapy for Parkinson's disease in the context of import substitution. Farmatsiya (Pharmacy). 2026. No. 2. pp. 60-67. (In Russ.)
3. KRISTELEVA D.A., GLEMBOTSKAYA G.T., SHINDRYAEVA N.N. Medical and social profile of the consumer of antiparkinsonian medications in the context of import substitution. Current Problems of Health Care and Medical Statistics. 2026. No. 2. pp. 39-51. (In Russ.)

# Digital Prescribing

## Project team

Head of the Department of Pharmacology, Candidate of Medical Sciences, Associate Professor E.A. Smolyarchuk; Professor, Doctor of Medical Sciences A.V. Samorodov; Associate Professor, Candidate of Biological Sciences S.S. Sologova; Associate Professor, Candidate of Medical Sciences E.A. Zavadich; Assistant D.A. Trashchenkova; Assistant E.V. Polikarpov

## Project description

## Objective

Development and evolution of the unified "Digital Formulation" digital platform, integrating training, simulation exercises, professional information sources, and skill mastery assessment into a single licensed product..

## Key points

- Personalization of training;
- Personalization of pharmacotherapy;
- Integration into the digital healthcare environment;
- Creation of a digital platform combining training, simulation exercises, professional information sources, and skills mastery assessment into a single licensed product;
- Development and implementation of a supplementary professional education program;

## Key research publications on the project

1. Tatzhikova, K. A., Poddubnaya, K. M., Vishnyakova, E. V., et al. Students’ Assessment of the Possibility of Implementing Innovative Teaching Methods Using Information and Communication Technologies at the Department of Pharmacology of Sechenov University. Pedagogical Informatics. 2023;4:440–458. ISSN 2070-9013.

